      "evidence_id": "pdfplumber:abstract",
      "source": "pdf_layout",
      "field": "abstract",
      "raw_text": "Stacked Garbling (SGC) is a Garbled Circuit (GC) improve- ment that efficiently and securely evaluates programs with conditional branching. SGC reduces bandwidth consumption such that communica- tion is proportional to the size of the single longest program execution path, rather than to the size of the entire program. Crucially, the parties expend increased computational effort compared to classic GC. Motivated by procuring a subset in a menu of computational services or tasks, we consider GC evaluation ofk-out-of-n branches, whose indices are known (or eventually revealed) to the GC evaluator E. Our stack- and-stagger technique amortizes GC computation in this setting. We retain the communication advantage of SGC, whilesignificantly improv- ing computation and wall-clock time . Namely, each GC party garbles (or evaluates) the total of n branches, a significant improvement over the O(n · k) garblings/evaluations needed by standard SGC. We present our construction as a garbling scheme. Our technique brings significant overall performance improvement in various settings, including those typically considered in the literature: e.g. on a 1Gbps LAN we evaluate 16-out-of-128 functions≈ 7.68× faster than standard stacked garbling.",
      "extractor": "pdfplumber_section_boundary"
    },
